In minimization, an old feasible incumbent value \(U_{\mathrm{old}}\) remains a valid upper bound, so pruning with \(L_v\geq U_{\mathrm{old}}-\varepsilon\) is safe but may be weaker than pruning with a newer \(U\). By contrast, a bound or cut derived for region \(D_{
obreak\mathrm{old}}\), model epoch \(e_{
obreak\mathrm{old}}\), or obsolete ownership cover cannot be applied to a different current region without recertification. Likewise, termination is unsound if stale load information overlooks nodes or messages in transit. Thus staleness is correctness-neutral only when the payload remains independently valid for its present use.
